WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.212
A well-structured launch still starts code
with real capabilities.

2
00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:08.837
By default, the child commonly inherits
the service's operating-system identity,

3
00:00:08.917 --> 00:00:12.566
accessible filesystem, network position,
selected environment,

4
00:00:12.646 --> 00:00:14.502
and configured standard streams.

5
00:00:14.582 --> 00:00:16.886
If the parent runs with broad permissions,

6
00:00:16.966 --> 00:00:21.319
a defect in the utility or an overly
powerful option can have broad consequences.

7
00:00:21.399 --> 00:00:24.343
Use the least practical privilege for the
service and,

8
00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:28.328
where risk warrants it, isolate the
operation with a dedicated account,

9
00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:32.696
constrained filesystem access, or an
existing sandbox or container boundary.

10
00:00:32.776 --> 00:00:34.952
Isolation must have an explicit policy;

11
00:00:35.032 --> 00:00:37.721
a container label alone is not a
permission model.

12
00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.401
Bound the work before launch.

13
00:00:39.481 --> 00:00:42.489
Validate file size, dimensions, duration,
page count,

14
00:00:42.569 --> 00:00:45.258
or other cost indicators relevant to the
utility.

15
00:00:45.338 --> 00:00:49.690
Limit concurrent children so request
bursts cannot exhaust processes, memory,

16
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.267
CPU, file descriptors, or temporary
storage.

17
00:00:52.347 --> 00:00:56.763
Place quotas at a shared server boundary
rather than only in one handler instance.

18
00:00:56.843 --> 00:01:01.132
Authorization and rate limits should
reflect that an external processor may be

19
00:01:01.212 --> 00:01:04.284
substantially more expensive than an
ordinary API call.

20
00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:08.845
Use asynchronous process APIs in
request-handling paths so the Node.js event loop

21
00:01:08.925 --> 00:01:10.717
can continue serving other work.

22
00:01:10.797 --> 00:01:14.958
Synchronous variants block the event loop
until the child exits and require a

23
00:01:15.038 --> 00:01:16.702
deliberate, bounded use case.

24
00:01:16.782 --> 00:01:18.958
Choose output handling intentionally.

25
00:01:19.038 --> 00:01:22.879
Exec-style APIs buffer stdout and stderr
and enforce a maximum buffer;

26
00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:25.839
understand that limit and treat overflow
as failure.

27
00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:30.335
Spawn can stream output, but the
application must apply backpressure, byte caps,

28
00:01:30.415 --> 00:01:34.832
and safe destinations rather than
accumulating unbounded data in memory or logs.

29
00:01:34.912 --> 00:01:38.881
Define a deadline with a timeout or
AbortSignal and handle cancellation,

30
00:01:38.961 --> 00:01:41.201
exit codes, signals, and partial output.

31
00:01:41.281 --> 00:01:45.825
Terminating the direct child may not
always stop descendants created by wrappers or

32
00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:50.514
shells, so test process-tree behavior on
supported platforms and use stronger job or

33
00:01:50.594 --> 00:01:52.578
isolation controls when necessary.

34
00:01:52.658 --> 00:01:56.179
Clean up temporary files on success,
failure, and cancellation.

35
00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:00.483
Timeouts, output bounds, and concurrency
controls do not prevent injection;

36
00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:05.108
they contain denial-of-service and
operational risk after a permitted process has

37
00:02:05.188 --> 00:02:05.700
started.
